# Data Flow of a Page Edit

This page follows a single edit through all three planes: the
[application](application-plane.xmd) plane (the editor and the bridge), the
[control](control-plane.xmd) plane (Kong gating access), and the
[data](data-plane.xmd) plane (Postgres and realtime). It ties the whole
architecture together in one concrete path.

## The journey of one keystroke

1. **You type.** osionos updates its in-memory block model and serializes the
   affected page.
2. **The client queues the write.** osionos does not fire a request per keystroke
   — it batches writes to be sent to the bridge.
3. **The bridge persists it.** `osionos-bridge` (`:4000`) receives the write and,
   holding the service-role key, forwards it to postgrest through Kong.
4. **Kong routes it.** `mini-baas-kong:8000` gates and forwards the request to
   postgrest.
5. **Postgres stores it.** postgrest writes the row into `mini-baas-postgres`
   under the `osionos_*` schema.
6. **Realtime fans it out.** A change notification propagates so other viewers of
   the page can update.

```text
   keystroke
      │  serialize block model
      ▼
   osionos (browser) ── batched write
      │
      ▼
   osionos-bridge (Node, holds SERVICE_ROLE_KEY)
      │  apikey + Bearer
      ▼
   mini-baas-kong:8000  ──►  postgrest  ──►  mini-baas-postgres
                                                   │
                                       change notification
                                                   ▼
                                   realtime (Rust) ──► other viewers
```

## Why the client batches

> [!warning] A write-per-keystroke storm is a real failure mode
> Sending one request per keystroke floods the bridge and Kong. Because the
> bridge is a single IP, a burst can trip Kong's rate limit and return `429`.
> The client batches writes and backs off on failure precisely to avoid this
> thundering herd.

## The realtime hop

The last step — fanning the change out to other viewers — is the Rust data
plane's realtime router. Postgres emits a change, a realtime producer picks it
up, and subscribers receive it.

> [!note] Payload size is bounded on the notify path
> The database change-notification channel is size-capped. Large pages send an
> **id-only** signal rather than the full row, so subscribers fetch the fresh
> content instead of receiving an oversized payload.

## Where each plane shows up

| Step | Plane | Component |
|:-----|:------|:----------|
| serialize + batch | application (TS) | osionos |
| persist with service key | application (TS) | osionos-bridge |
| gate + route | control | Kong |
| store | data | postgrest → Postgres |
| fan-out | data (Rust) | realtime router |

## Recap

- [x] The edit never leaves the browser with a service key — the bridge holds it.
- [x] Every write crosses Kong, the single front door.
- [x] The realtime hop is what makes the workspace *collaborative*.

> [!tip] Zoom back out
> This one flow touches every idea in this section — the [three
> planes](three-language-planes.xmd), the [bridge boundary](bridge-trust-boundary.xmd),
> and the [container-DNS topology](container-dns-topology.xmd).
